This file's presence, not its contents, is the whole message: s6-supervise
starts a service whose directory contains a file called `down` in the DOWN
state instead of starting it. s6 never opens the file, so the reasoning can
live in it rather than in a comment somewhere else that would drift away from
it. (The gate checks that too, with this text in place, because a fact this
file depends on is worth measuring rather than believing.)

WHY NGINX SHIPS DOWN. The gate on the web stack is MOD_WEB in anvil.conf. It
is read AT RUNTIME, on the printer, from a file the user edits over ssh -- and
this payload is built ONCE, weeks earlier, by bin/patch.sh. So the scandir
cannot be where the decision is made: a service directory that ships without
this file is a service the scanner starts a fraction of a second after
init.d/S40s6 runs, before anything has read anvil.conf and regardless of what
it says. MOD_WEB=0 would mean "nginx runs for two seconds on every boot and is
then shot", which is not what it says on the tin and is worse than not having
the switch.

So the shipped state is "down", meaning: THE SCANNER DOES NOT DECIDE WHEN
NGINX STARTS. init.d/S60nginx does, at the point in the boot where anvil.conf
has been sourced, by calling svc_s6_up -- which overrides this file for as
long as that s6-supervise lives. MOD_WEB=1 and it comes up there; MOD_WEB=0
and it never comes up at all, which is exactly what the switch has always
promised. The same is then true of every other way in: `S60nginx start` over
ssh re-reads anvil.conf, and a user who sets MOD_WEB=0 and reboots gets no web
server rather than a web server that has to be caught and stopped.

THE COST, stated honestly, because it is the one thing this arrangement gives
up. `down` is consulted when s6-supervise STARTS, not when the service dies:
once S60nginx has brought nginx up, s6 keeps restarting it on every crash for
as long as that supervisor lives, which is the entire point of the migration
and is unaffected by this file. What it does mean is that if s6-supervise
ITSELF is killed -- not nginx, the supervisor -- s6-svscan starts a fresh one,
that fresh one reads this file, and nginx stays down until something runs
`S60nginx start` again. That is a rare failure (s6-supervise is a small C
program that blocks in poll() and is not the thing that crashes) with an
honest signal (`S60nginx status` says it is not running, and says so from
s6-svstat rather than from a pidfile) and a one-line fix from an ssh session.
The alternative -- deleting this file at runtime once MOD_WEB has been read --
buys back that corner case at the price of a payload that rewrites its own
install tree, which every path in this mod is arranged not to do.

This file is not a record of anything having gone wrong: it is the state nginx
ships in, on every build, and nothing removes it.
